---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/biotin`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Biotin (Vitamin B7) Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Biotin', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Biotin (Vitamin B7): Hair, Nails, and the Lab Test Interference Warning"
  description="Biotin reference — actual evidence for hair and nail claims, the FDA warning about biotin interfering with troponin and thyroid lab tests, and safe dosing."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/biotin", label: "Biotin" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Biotin (Vitamin B7)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Biotin is a cofactor for four carboxylase enzymes central to fatty acid, amino acid,
      and carbohydrate metabolism. It's marketed heavily as a "hair, skin, and nails"
      supplement — a claim that is largely unsupported outside of true deficiency, and
      that comes with a real lab-test interference risk at supplement doses.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily Adequate Intake (AI)</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 30 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 30 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 35 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>No Tolerable Upper Intake Level</strong> — biotin has no known toxicity, but see lab-interference warning below.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2">
      Note that a typical "hair, skin, and nails" supplement contains <strong>5,000–10,000 µg</strong>
      — that's 167–333× the AI.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The hair and nails evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Biotin deficiency causes hair loss and brittle nails — but true deficiency is rare
      (raw egg whites contain avidin, which binds biotin; long-term valproate or isotretinoin
      use can lower biotin). In people who are NOT deficient, RCT evidence for hair or nail
      growth is essentially absent. The perceived effect in advertising is placebo plus
      correction of undiagnosed marginal deficiency in a subset of consumers.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The FDA lab interference warning</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      In 2017 and again in 2019, the FDA warned that biotin at supplement doses (&gt; 1 mg)
      interferes with many biotin-streptavidin-based lab assays, producing:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Falsely low troponin</strong> — potentially causing missed diagnosis of heart attack.</li>
      <li><strong>Falsely low TSH and falsely high T4/T3</strong> — mimicking hyperthyroidism.</li>
      <li><strong>Falsely elevated hCG</strong> — false-positive pregnancy or unreliable oncology monitoring.</li>
      <li><strong>Falsely low PTH, ferritin, various tumour markers.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      Stop biotin supplements at least <strong>72 hours</strong> before blood work, and tell
      the phlebotomist and ordering clinician about the supplement.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Beef liver (3 oz): 30.8 µg — the AI in a single serving</li>
      <li>Egg, cooked (1 large): 10 µg</li>
      <li>Salmon (3 oz): 5 µg</li>
      <li>Sunflower seeds (1 oz): 2.6 µg</li>
      <li>Sweet potato (½ cup): 2.4 µg</li>
      <li>Almonds (1 oz): 1.5 µg</li>
      <li>Milk (1 cup): 0.3 µg</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      A varied omnivorous diet supplies the AI easily. Biotin is also synthesised by gut
      bacteria, contributing to total availability.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If you're on a "hair growth" biotin dose and
      about to have blood tests, stop the supplement 3 days beforehand — this matters most
      for suspected heart attack, thyroid workup, and oncology labs.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/biotin" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
